WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
your traditional ABC inventory analysis,

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:03.960
it's lying to you.

3
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.800
It's quietly hiding the exact products

4
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.440
that actually make you money.

5
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.560
Let's uncover the C-Class trap in this quick explainer.

6
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.560
Picture this, you've got a high value SKU

7
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.680
that sells maybe one or two units a month.

8
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:15.920
Because of that low volume,

9
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.080
your ABC analysis calls it C-Class,

10
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.000
so it basically gets ignored.

11
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.240
But then, national day hits,

12
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.320
and suddenly it sells 18 units.

13
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:25.960
Literally half of its annual demand

14
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.600
drops in one single week.

15
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.040
In your flat seasonal index,

16
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.760
it completely buries that massive spike.

17
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.400
And this is exactly why the old system breaks down.

18
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.760
Look, ABC only ranks by annual volume.

19
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.880
It is totally blind to the actual demand pattern.

20
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.200
That's what XYZ analysis measures,

21
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.760
the coefficient of variation,

22
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:46.880
meaning it actually tells you

23
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.600
how erratic that demand truly is.

24
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.400
If you're still relying on an outdated three-class system,

25
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.400
you are ignoring the most dangerous zone

26
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.520
in your entire supply chain, the CZ Quadrant.

27
00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.560
That is low C-Class volume

28
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.680
combined with a highly erratic Z pattern.

29
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.840
It's an absolute trap for stockouts and dead capital,

30
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.480
yet almost nobody manages it separately.

31
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.320
So here's the fix.

32
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.160
You have to segment on ABC and XYZ together.

33
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.400
You create a clean nine-zone matrix,

34
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.480
not just three broad classes.

35
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.160
Pull those CZ items out

36
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.840
and give them event-driven replenishment

37
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.080
tied directly to the GCC calendar,

38
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.760
not some completely useless monthly average.

39
00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.240
The real-world payoff for this is huge.

40
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.400
This exact nine-zone strategy

41
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.400
eliminated a massive 18% of age stock at Ans Dubai.

42
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.440
And the best part?

43
00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.720
Service on those unpredictable tail-end items

44
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:40.360
actually improved.

45
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:43.960
This is how operators like Vinayak Bhadani out in Dubai

46
00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.600
handle supply chain uncertainty

47
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.440
without all the corporate fluff.

48
00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.800
So I'll leave you with this.

49
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.840
Is your current basic inventory matrix

50
00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.200
secretly destroying your margins right now?
